From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 04:46:35 +0000
Subject: inspector: only dispatch interrupt-safe methods from V8 interrupts

Messages for a main thread session are dispatched from a V8 API
interrupt while JavaScript runs. V8 no longer allows protocol dispatch
to run JavaScript there
(https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/v8/v8/+/8775587), and
Electron builds with v8_disallow_js_in_api_interrupts_is_checked, so a
method that runs JavaScript, such as Runtime.evaluate or
Runtime.callFunctionOn sent while the main thread is busy, aborted the
process with "Invoke in DisallowJavascriptExecutionScope".

Like Blink's ShouldInterruptForMethod(), classify each message when it
is posted to the thread and only dispatch an allow-list of methods that
never run JavaScript from the interrupt: the ones Blink allows that
Node.js implements, the DOMStorage reads that were made safe for
interrupts, Storage.getStorageKey and NodeWorker.sendMessageToWorker.
Keep any other message queued, together with every later request for
the same session so that a session sees its messages handled in order,
and dispatch them once the thread yields to the event loop or enters a
pause. Other sessions are not held up. Debugger.pause and
Runtime.terminateExecution exist to interrupt the thread, so like on
Blink's IO channel they are dispatched even when earlier messages of
the session are deferred.

The deferred requests are dispatched from an immediate that keeps the
event loop alive, or from a thread-safe immediate when JavaScript runs
before the libuv handles exist, as while a Worker bootstraps.
WaitForFrontendEvent() dispatches queued requests instead of waiting,
because no interrupt is pending for them.

Known limitation: as in Chrome, Runtime.enable and Debugger.enable are
not on the allow-list, so a frontend that attaches to a process that is
already stuck in JavaScript cannot pause it, since Debugger.pause fails
while Debugger.enable is deferred.

